---->| * | *-+---->| * | *-+---->| * | *-+---->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+     +-|-+---+     +-|-+---+     +-|-+---+
       |             |             |             |
       V             V             V             V
     +---+         +---+         +---+         +---+
     | 1 |         | 2 |         | 3 |         | 4 |
     +---+         +---+         +---+         +---+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap2/Fig2.4e,,125mm,Figure 2.4: The sequence 1@comma{} 2@comma{} 3@comma{} 4 represented as a chain of pairs.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap2/Fig2.4e.std,,125mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 2.4:} The sequence 1, 2, 3, 4 represented as a chain of pairs.}
@end float

@noindent
Such a sequence of pairs, formed by nested @code{cons}es, is called a
@newterm{list}.  This edition builds it as the generic union
@code{List<A>}: a value is either the empty list @code{nil} or a cons cell
carrying a head and the rest of the list, so a helper constructor builds the
chain in one expression.@footnote{In this book, we use @newterm{list} to mean a
chain of pairs terminated by the end-of-list marker.  In contrast, the term
@newterm{list structure} refers to any data structure made out of pairs, not
just to lists.}  The above sequence could be produced by
@code{list(1, 2, 3, 4)}.  In general,

@example typescript
list(⟨@var{a₁}⟩, ⟨@var{a₂}⟩, @dots{}, ⟨@var{aₙ}⟩)
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example typescript
cons(⟨@var{a₁}⟩,
     cons(⟨@var{a₂}⟩,
          cons(@dots{},
               cons(⟨@var{aₙ}⟩, nil))))
@end example

@noindent
Lisp systems conventionally print lists by printing the sequence of elements,
enclosed in parentheses.  This edition's @code{showList} renders a list the
same way, so the data object in @ref{Figure 2.4} displays as @code{(1 2 3 4)}:

@example typescript
const oneThroughFour = list(1, 2, 3, 4);

showList(oneThroughFour)
// => "(1 2 3 4)"
@end example

@noindent
Be careful not to confuse the expression @code{list(1, 2, 3, 4)} with the list
@code{(1 2 3 4)}, which is the string @code{showList} renders from the value
the expression evaluates to.  Scheme blurs the distinction because an
unevaluated expression can itself be taken as data; TypeScript draws the line
in the type system, and the list of @ref{Figure 2.4} is a value of type
@code{List<number>}, never a piece of program.

We can think of @code{car} as selecting the first item in the list, and of
@code{cdr} as selecting the sublist consisting of all but the first item.
Nested applications of @code{car} and @code{cdr} can be used to extract the
second, third, and subsequent items in the list.@footnote{Since nested
applications of @code{car} and @code{cdr} are cumbersome to write, Lisp
dialects provide abbreviations for them---for instance, the @code{cadr} of a
list is the @code{car} of its @code{cdr}.  The names of all such procedures
start with @code{c} and end with @code{r}, and each @code{a} or @code{d}
between them stands for one selection.  TypeScript provides no such
abbreviations, and because the empty list has no first item, the edition's
@code{car} and @code{cdr} answer the option of the item rather than the item
itself; the recursive listings below narrow on the list's two shapes instead,
which reads better than any chain of abbreviations.} The constructor
@code{cons} makes a list like the original one, but with an additional item at
the beginning.

@example typescript
car(oneThroughFour)
// => @{ _tag: "Some", value: 1 @}

showList(getOrElse(cdr(oneThroughFour), nil))
// => "(2 3 4)"

car(getOrElse(cdr(oneThroughFour), nil))
// => @{ _tag: "Some", value: 2 @}

showList(cons(10, oneThroughFour))
// => "(10 1 2 3 4)"

showList(cons(5, oneThroughFour))
// => "(5 1 2 3 4)"
@end example

@noindent
The value of @code{nil}, used to terminate the chain of pairs, can be thought
of as a sequence of no elements, the @newterm{empty list}.  The word
@newterm{nil} is a contraction of the Latin word @emph{nihil}, which means
``nothing.''@footnote{It's remarkable how much energy in the standardization of
Lisp dialects has been dissipated in arguments that are literally over nothing:
Should @code{nil} be an ordinary name?  Should the value of @code{nil} be a
symbol?  Should it be a list?  Should it be a pair?  This edition sidesteps
the entire issue the way the languages born after the brawls do: the empty
list is the union's @code{Nil} variant, bound to the name @code{nil}, and the
type system knows which values are lists and which are not.  Once we have
introduced quotation in @ref{2.3}, we will denote the empty list as
@code{'()} and dispense with the variable entirely.}

@subsubheading List operations

The use of pairs to represent sequences of elements as lists is accompanied by
conventional programming techniques for manipulating lists by successively
``@code{cdr}ing down'' the lists.  For example, the procedure @code{listRef}
takes as arguments a list and a number @math{n} and returns the
@math{n^{\hbox{th}}} item of the list.  It is customary to number the elements
of the list beginning with 0.  The method for computing @code{listRef} is the
following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
For @math{{n = 0}}, @code{listRef} should return the @code{car} of the list.

@item
Otherwise, @code{listRef} should return  the @math{{(n - 1)}}-st item of the
@code{cdr} of the list.

@end itemize

@example typescript
const listRef = <A>(items: List<A>, n: number): Option<A> => @{
  if (n === 0) @{
    return car(items);
  @}
  const rest = cdr(items);
  return rest._tag === "Some" ? listRef(rest.value, n - 1) : none;
@};

const squares = list(1, 4, 9, 16, 25);

listRef(squares, 3)
// => @{ _tag: "Some", value: 16 @}
@end example

@noindent
Often we @code{cdr} down the whole list.  To aid in this, the edition provides
the predicate @code{isNull}, which tests whether its argument is the empty
list.  The procedure @code{length}, which returns the number of items in a
list, illustrates this typical pattern of use:

@example typescript
const length = <A>(items: List<A>): number =>
  items._tag === "Nil" ? 0 : 1 + length(items.tail);

const odds = list(1, 3, 5, 7);

length(odds)
// => 4
@end example

@noindent
The @code{length} procedure implements a simple recursive plan. The reduction
step is:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{length} of any list is 1 plus the @code{length} of the @code{cdr} of
the list.

@end itemize

@noindent
This is applied successively until we reach the base case:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{length} of the empty list is 0.

@end itemize

@noindent
We could also compute @code{length} in an iterative style, which this edition
expresses as a loop:

@example typescript
const lengthIter = <A>(items: List<A>): number => @{
  let count = 0;
  for (let rest = items; rest._tag === "Cons"; rest = rest.tail) @{
    count += 1;
  @}
  return count;
@};
@end example

@noindent
Another conventional programming technique is to ``@code{cons} up'' an answer
list while @code{cdr}ing down a list, as in the procedure @code{append}, which
takes two lists as arguments and combines their elements to make a new list:

@example typescript
showList(append(squares, odds))
// => "(1 4 9 16 25 1 3 5 7)"

showList(append(odds, squares))
// => "(1 3 5 7 1 4 9 16 25)"
@end example

@noindent
@code{Append} is also implemented using a recursive plan.  To @code{append}
lists @code{list1} and @code{list2}, do the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
If @code{list1} is the empty list, then the result is just @code{list2}.

@item
Otherwise, @code{append} the @code{cdr} of @code{list1} and @code{list2}, and
@code{cons} the @code{car} of @code{list1} onto the result:

@end itemize

@example typescript
const append = <A>(list1: List<A>, list2: List<A>): List<A> =>
  list1._tag === "Nil" ? list2 : cons(list1.head, append(list1.tail, list2));
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.17}Exercise 2.17:} Define a procedure
@code{lastPair} that returns the list that contains only the last element of a
given (nonempty) list:

@example typescript
showList(lastPair(list(23, 72, 149, 34)))
// => "(34)"
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.18}Exercise 2.18:} Define a procedure @code{reverse}
that takes a list as argument and returns a list of the same elements in
reverse order:

@example typescript
showList(reverse(list(1, 4, 9, 16, 25)))
// => "(25 16 9 4 1)"
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.19}Exercise 2.19:} Consider the change-counting
program of @ref{1.2.2}.  It would be nice to be able to easily change
the currency used by the program, so that we could compute the number of ways
to change a British pound, for example.  As the program is written, the
knowledge of the currency is distributed partly into the procedure
@code{firstDenomination} and partly into the procedure @code{cc}
(which knows that there are five kinds of U.S. coins).  It would be nicer to be
able to supply a list of coins to be used for making change.

We want to rewrite the procedure @code{cc} so that its second argument is a
list of the values of the coins to use rather than an integer specifying which
coins to use.  We could then have lists that defined each kind of currency:

@example typescript
const usCoins = list(50, 25, 10, 5, 1);

const ukCoins = list(100, 50, 20, 10, 5, 2, 1, 0.5);
@end example

We could then call @code{cc} as follows:

@example typescript
cc(100, usCoins)
// => 292
@end example

To do this will require changing the program @code{cc} somewhat.  It will still
have the same form, but it will access its second argument differently, as
follows:

@example typescript
const cc = (amount: number, coinValues: List<number>): number =>
  amount === 0
    ? 1
    : amount < 0 || noMore(coinValues)
      ? 0
      : cc(amount, exceptFirstDenomination(coinValues)) +
        cc(amount - firstDenomination(coinValues), coinValues);
@end example

Define the procedures @code{firstDenomination},
@code{exceptFirstDenomination} and @code{noMore} in terms of primitive
operations on list structures.  Does the order of the list @code{coinValues}
affect the answer produced by @code{cc}?  Why or why not?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.20}Exercise 2.20:} The procedures @code{+},
@code{*}, and @code{list} take arbitrary numbers of arguments.  Scheme spells
such definitions with @newterm{dotted-tail notation}: a parameter list with a
dot before the final name, whose final parameter is bound to a @newterm{list}
of the remaining arguments.  TypeScript spells the same mechanism with a
@newterm{rest parameter}: a parameter whose name is preceded by @code{...}
collects all remaining arguments into an array.  For instance, given the
definition

@example typescript
const f = (x: number, y: number, ...z: ReadonlyArray<number>) => ⟨@var{body}⟩;
@end example

@noindent
the procedure @code{f} can be called with two or more arguments.  If we
evaluate

@example typescript
f(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6)
@end example

@noindent
then in the body of @code{f}, @code{x} will be 1, @code{y} will be 2, and
@code{z} will be the array @code{[3, 4, 5, 6]}.  Given the definition

@example typescript
const g = (...w: ReadonlyArray<number>) => ⟨@var{body}⟩;
@end example

@noindent
the procedure @code{g} can be called with zero or more arguments.  If we
evaluate

@example typescript
g(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6)
@end example

@noindent
then in the body of @code{g}, @code{w} will be the array @code{[1, 2, 3, 4, 5,
6]}.@footnote{The book's @code{define} with a rest parameter is the edition's
arrow function with one; the two spellings above are those arrow functions.}

Use rest parameters to write a procedure @code{sameParity} that takes one or
more integers and returns a list of all the arguments that have the same
even-odd parity as the first argument.  For example,

@example typescript
showList(sameParity(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7))
// => "(1 3 5 7)"

showList(sameParity(2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7))
// => "(2 4 6)"
@end example
@end quotation

@subsubheading Mapping over lists

One extremely useful operation is to apply some transformation to each element
in a list and generate the list of results.  For instance, the following
procedure scales each number in a list by a given factor:

@example typescript
const scaleList = (items: List<number>, factor: number): List<number> =>
  items._tag === "Nil" ? nil : cons(items.head * factor, scaleList(items.tail, factor));

showList(scaleList(list(1, 2, 3, 4, 5), 10))
// => "(10 20 30 40 50)"
@end example

@noindent
We can abstract this general idea and capture it as a common pattern expressed
as a higher-order procedure, just as in @ref{1.3}.  The higher-order
procedure here is called @code{map}.  @code{Map} takes as arguments a procedure
of one argument and a list, and returns a list of the results produced by
applying the procedure to each element in the list:@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 78}The host's arrays
carry a @code{map} method of their own, more general than the one described
here: it takes a procedure of @math{n} arguments together with @math{n}
arrays.  The @code{List} union is not an array, so the edition builds the
book's map for it; the chapter's listings use only the unary map shown here.}

@example typescript
const map = <A, B>(f: (x: A) => B, items: List<A>): List<B> =>
  items._tag === "Nil" ? nil : cons(f(items.head), map(f, items.tail));

showList(map(Math.abs, list(-10, 2.5, -11.6, 17)))
// => "(10 2.5 11.6 17)"

showList(map((x) => x * x, list(1, 2, 3, 4)))
// => "(1 4 9 16)"
@end example

@noindent
Now we can give a new definition of @code{scale-list} in terms of @code{map}:

@example typescript
const scaleList = (items: List<number>, factor: number): List<number> =>
  map((x) => x * factor, items);
@end example

@noindent
@code{Map} is an important construct, not only because it captures a common
pattern, but because it establishes a higher level of abstraction in dealing
with lists.  In the original definition of @code{scale-list}, the recursive
structure of the program draws attention to the element-by-element processing
of the list.  Defining @code{scale-list} in terms of @code{map} suppresses that
level of detail and emphasizes that scaling transforms a list of elements to a
list of results.  The difference between the two definitions is not that the
computer is performing a different process (it isn't) but that we think about
the process differently.  In effect, @code{map} helps establish an abstraction
barrier that isolates the implementation of procedures that transform lists
from the details of how the elements of the list are extracted and combined.
Like the barriers shown in @ref{Figure 2.1}, this abstraction gives us the
flexibility to change the low-level details of how sequences are implemented,
while preserving the conceptual framework of operations that transform
sequences to sequences.  Section @ref{2.2.3} expands on this use of sequences
as a framework for organizing programs.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.21}Exercise 2.21:} The procedure @code{squareList}
takes a list of numbers as argument and returns a list of the squares of those
numbers.

@example typescript
showList(squareList(list(1, 2, 3, 4)))
// => "(1 4 9 16)"
@end example

Here are two different definitions of @code{square-list}.  Complete both of
them by filling in the missing expressions:

@example typescript
const squareList = (items: List<number>): List<number> =>
  items._tag === "Nil" ? nil : cons(⟨??⟩, ⟨??⟩));

const squareList = (items: List<number>): List<number> => map(⟨??⟩, ⟨??⟩);
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.22}Exercise 2.22:} Louis Reasoner tries to rewrite
the first @code{square-list} procedure of @ref{Exercise 2.21} so that it
evolves an iterative process:

@example typescript
const squareList = (items: List<number>): List<number> => @{
  const iter = (things: List<number>, answer: List<number>): List<number> =>
    things._tag === "Nil"
      ? answer
      : iter(things.tail, cons(things.head * things.head, answer));
  return iter(items, nil);
@};
@end example

Unfortunately, defining @code{square-list} this way produces the answer list in
the reverse order of the one desired.  Why?

Louis then tries to fix his bug by interchanging the arguments to @code{cons}:

@example typescript
const squareList = (items: List<number>): List<number> => @{
  const iter = (things: List<number>, answer: List<number>): List<number> =>
    things._tag === "Nil"
      ? answer
      : iter(things.tail, cons(answer, things.head * things.head));
  return iter(items, nil);
@};
@end example

This doesn't work either.  Explain.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.23}Exercise 2.23:} The procedure @code{forEach} is
similar to @code{map}.  It takes as arguments a procedure and a list of
elements.  However, rather than forming a list of the results, @code{forEach}
just applies the procedure to each of the elements in turn, from left to right.
The values returned by applying the procedure to the elements are not used at
all---@code{forEach} is used with procedures that perform an action, such as
printing.  For example,

@example typescript
forEach((x) => @{
  process.stdout.write(`$@{x@}\n`);
@}, list(57, 321, 88))
// 57
// 321
// 88
@end example

The value returned by the call to @code{forEach} (not illustrated above) can
be something arbitrary, such as true.  Give an implementation of
@code{forEach}.
@end quotation

@node	2.2.2, 2.2.3, 2.2.1, 2.2
@subsection Hierarchical Structures

The representation of sequences in terms of lists generalizes naturally to
represent sequences whose elements may themselves be sequences.  For example,
we can regard the object @code{((1 2) 3 4)} constructed by

@example typescript
cons(list(1, 2), list(3, 4));
@end example

@noindent
as a list of three items, the first of which is itself a list, @code{(1 2)}.
Indeed, this is suggested by the form in which @code{showList} renders the
value.  @ref{Figure 2.5} shows the representation of
this structure in terms of pairs.

@float
@anchor{Figure 2.5}
@ifinfo
@quotation
